%TAG !u! tag:unity3d.com,2011:
--- !u!114 &11400000
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 99e27739e1e77c341b172c046cd86111, type: 3}
  m_Name: GearAssetFitProfile_gear_shield_ironwood
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: GalaQuest.Gear.Editor::GalaQuest.Gear.Editor.GearAssetFitProfile
  semanticAssetId: gear.shield.ironwood
  slot: 4
  rawToCanonicalEuler: {x: 90, y: 0, z: 0}
  orientationProvenance: 2
  orientationNote: "AUTHORED: source board lies in raw XZ with its thin axis on raw Y; +90 X maps the board normal to canonical forward. See the source observation in cavityNote; not an inferred production rotation."
  cavitySource: 2
  cavityCenterInCanonical: {x: 0, y: 0, z: 0}
  cavitySizeInCanonical: {x: 0.01, y: 0.01, z: 0.0017320508}
  cavityProvenance: 2
  cavityNote: 'AUTHORED functional envelope, not a measured cavity. The source art
    declares no GQ_FIT_CAVITY locator, and a shield encloses no anatomy, so there
    is no interior shell to measure and none is invented here. What is authored is
    the DECISION that this board IS the functional envelope for fit purposes; the
    extents themselves are the board the source mesh presents, in canonical asset
    space after the raw-to-canonical rotation below. Raw-to-canonical is AUTHORED
    from a human observation about the source art: the exported board lies flat,
    with its thinnest axis on raw +Y, so +90 degrees about X stands it upright with
    its face on canonical +Z (wearer forward). The source FBX is also authored at
    roughly one centimetre across, which is why normalization against the Hero
    produces a large uniform scalar; that is a source unit convention, not a fit
    decision.'
  landmarks:
  - landmarkId: ASSET_FIT_GRIP
    positionInCanonical: {x: 0, y: 0, z: -0.0008660254}
    provenance: 2
    note: 'AUTHORED carry convention, stated in words before any number: the Ironwood
      Shield is a round board with a central boss, and a round centre-boss shield is
      carried on a centre grip directly behind that boss. So the wearer hand sits at
      the board centre, on its rear face. In canonical asset space that is x=0, y=0
      (board centre) and z = -half the board thickness (the rear face, since the
      board face is on canonical +Z). No grip geometry exists in the source mesh to
      measure, so this is authored intent and is NOT recorded as a measurement. If
      the source art later ships a GQ_FIT_GRIP locator, that measured landmark
      should replace this.'
